hi there steve kaufman here today i want  to talk about um  when should we start speaking in a  language and i refer specific to this  concept that we need to speak from day  one we need to speak early in order to  learn a language that this is somehow  a shortcut  a secret to  learning languages more quickly  i mentioned this because we had a  and still have an interesting thread at  our forum at link  on the subject of when we should start  speaking and it started with one of our  members who is a great contributor to  link evgeny who has created countless  wonderful lessons for learning russian  in our library and who's a very  experienced teacher and he says he gets  annoyed  at those students who feel they can just  talk the rate of fluency and don't put  the effort into listening and reading  and building up their vocabulary and  learning the grammar and so forth and so  on  and this sparked a lively debate and  some people agreeing with them some  people not  i of course agree with evgeny i'm not a  teacher so i'm not bothered by  people who won't put the effort into  learning the language we just somehow  think they can magically  talk their way to fluency  but i speak more as a learner  and i'm trying to remember the points  that i had made  basically summarizing the discussion  there's three things wrong with this  idea of you know speak from day one  speak my way to fluency  the first one is that it's not very  practical  if you have no words and if you don't  understand what the other person is  saying you can't say very much  so  speaking from day one means either  having a very  patient  person who's going to accommodate you  while you essentially have  nothing to say and don't understand what  they're saying  or paying for a teacher  most people who study a language are not  in the country  so they're somewhere else so they would  have to i guess get a teacher or  you know try and find some native  speakers who are going to spend time  with you but realistically how do you  feel if someone comes up to you who can  barely speak english and says i want to  speak to you in english  i quite frankly unless this is a close  friend or unless they're paying me  i'm not going to spend much time with  them maybe i'll be polite for a couple  of minutes but nothing much beyond that  uh  if we are in a country where we don't  speak the language we're better off to  use english for a practical  for practical reasons so therefore i  think it's not realistic to expect that  you can speak from day one  uh  the second thing is that it is  irrelevant to the final outcome in terms  of if our goal is to achieve fluency  whether we speak from day one day 10 day  50 it doesn't matter in my experience i  like to start speaking when i have  something to say in other words when i  have enough words that i can i can  actually have a meaningful and therefore  enjoyable  uh and i have seen all kinds of examples  uh when i lived in japan for example of  people who had a few phrases  uh you know hello goodbye you know dame  no good  utsugushi beautiful or subarashi or  konnichiwa and that's all they ever  learned  those people who became fluent in the  language put the effort into  listening reading building vocabulary  studying you know books i mean it takes  work  and just kind of hoping that you can  converse your way to fluency i think is  therefore a unrealistic b irrelevant to  the final outcome  and the third thing is  that  by encouraging people to think that they  can just talk the rate of fluency it it  hides the fact that it actually is a  fair amount of work to achieve fluency  in my own experience like i read  thousands hundreds of thousands of words  and i listen to most of that now i enjoy  doing that and that's the other thing  uh a lot of the sort of  passive learning if you want is for many  people  almost the goal of their learning it's  extremely enjoyable we like to watch  movies in french or japanese or read  you know or watch korean drama  or  you know even to be able to understand  the conversation  uh  this this ability to  understand what's going on in other  words to have the vocabulary and to  build up on comprehension  is  rewarding it's extremely rewarding and  it is the precondition for fluency you  can't become fluent if you don't  understand what other people are saying  to you so i mentioned this first of all  uh because of the  uh discussion we had on our forum and  also because it relates to the 90-day  challenge that i'm doing in korea and  people have asked me  you know because i'm going to be doing  videos  uh both in english and korean talking  about what i do how i'm learning how i  spend my time during the day and also  talking to korean people when the  opportunity is there  but there's no obligation for other  people to do this  people can take the 90-day challenge  commit to spending time  with the language reading and listening  and they can talk as little or as much  as they want and they certainly have no  obligation to produce a video for other  people to see  however i will say  that  uh in our path to fluency eventually we  have to talk a lot you cannot become  fluent you cannot convert your passive  knowledge of the language into fluency  unless you expose yourself to a lot of  situations where you are speaking but  that comes later on but one of the very  useful things to do is to create videos  on youtube and keep them private you  don't have to publicize them and then  you can  as you watch yourself speak you become  very much aware of where your mistakes  are and what you need to work on to  improve so  you know there's a lot of different  strategies you can use to get yourself  to where uh you know you can speak well  but  speaking from day one is not one of them  so  insofar as my own activities now in  korean  uh i have already spent  you know probably six seven eight months  on average an hour a day so whatever  that is 150 200 hours  on korean so i'm not starting from  scratch and yet when i go to speak to  people  you know if i see some korean speakers  in vancouver i will not pass up the uh  pass up the opportunity but i go to them  more to show off than anything else  because i don't understand what they're  saying back to me very well i haven't  been in very many practical situations  of communicating occasionally when i  play golf with my friend mr choi will  speak in korean i don't understand much  of what he's saying he has an accent  that's another issue  uh  you know i have like when i was really  into my career six seven years ago i did  have some meetings  with the president of a local korean  newspaper and we were half an english  half in korean but my korean has  since declined so my strategy here again  is i've got to get my korean up to a  level where i can understand what people  are saying and so the initial 90 days  okay the initial 15 to  30 days is going to be heavily focused  on input and thereafter i will step up  the speaking and i intend also to do  more writing which i haven't done in the  past so i'm tomorrow which is the first  day of my 90-day challenge i'm going to  go to a korean bookstore here in town  and i'm going to buy a book which  hopefully which korean children use  to learn to write the hangul  because i'm going to have to write it if  i'm going to pass this  topic exam sometime in april when i  intend to go to  go to korea but in any case i just  wanted to mention that that at least  from my perspective  you start speaking when you feel like  speaking there's no obligation if you  want to try to speak if the opportunity  is there you want to try it what you've  learned do so but if you don't have that  opportunity and you're not going to  rearrange your social life just to  create that opportunity  it's not a problem  many people have a few phrases that's  all they ever learn and other people who  don't use those phrases but start  speaking a month or six months later  they may end up with an even higher  level like it's largely irrelevant so  there i i wanted to express these views  and again tomorrow i start my korean
 

